  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM EST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight...Isolated to scattered showers, and possibly a \nstorm or two will develop through early this evening, mainly across \nthe interior with a diffuse inland moving east coast sea breeze. \nHowever, rain chances remain on the low side (around 20-30%). Into \ntonight, a weak onshore flow may still be able to generate a few \nshowers that can reach the coast, mainly south of the Cape. There \nare some low probabilities (30% or less) for patchy fog to develop \nlate tonight across the interior as onshore winds weaken, and have \nadded it to the forecast from around Orlando area north. Should any \nfog form, visibilities will improve past daybreak Thursday. Lows \ntonight will fall into the upper 60s to low 70s. \n  \nThursday-Friday...Weak front moving into the southeast U.S. late \nthis week will weaken the influence of the area of high pressure \noffshore the eastern U.S. seaboard. This will decrease onshore winds \nacross the region, with E/NE winds on Thursday only around 5-10 mph \nand then winds will become somewhat light and variable into Friday. \nDrier air gradually moves in, with mostly rain-free conditions \nforecast through late week. There may be a few showers that can \nstill develop mainly near to northwest of the I-4 corridor. However, \nmost areas look to remain dry, which will help floodwaters across \nsouthern Brevard County and the Treasure Coast to continue to \ngradually recede. However, residents and visitors should continue to \navoid any flooded roadways and remain out of any lingering \nfloodwaters.  \n\nHighs will remain warmer than normal in the mid 80s through late \nweek, with overnight lows mostly in the mid to upper 60s.  \n\nSaturday-Tuesday...A weak cool front pushes through Central Florida \ninto Saturday. Moisture with this boundary remains limited and \ntherefore looks to be a mostly dry frontal passage. Winds shift out \nof the north-northwest Saturday and then gradually veer onshore \nthrough late weekend into early next week as high pressure builds in \nbehind this boundary. The forecast keeps rain chances rather low (10-\n20%) through much of the period, but there is increasing uncertainty \nin the overall pattern into early next week as significant \ndifferences remain between the models. The GFS still shows a robust \ncut-off low aloft across the southern U.S shifting east, developing \nan area of low pressure over the Gulf. This system moves NE and \npushes a cold front (leading to wetter/stormier conditions) across \nthe area on Tuesday. However, the ECMWF and Canadian models keeps \nthe mid level feature weaker, delaying any rain chances until the \nmiddle of next week. Currently the forecast leans toward the \nECMWF/Canadian, but will have to continue to monitor trends in \nfuture model runs. Temperatures trend cooler, closer to more normal \nvalues for this time of year by late weekend into early next week, \nwith highs in the upper 70 to low 80s and lows in the 60s \n(potentially upper 50s NW of I-4).",
